I think the lesson we learn from Marvin's record is that, while you _can_
lose from bad luck, almost all the deaths you feel are such are actually
lack of skill.

For example, even hill orcs have a MONSTR of 4; you'd have to be XL 5 for
that to happen. Why so high an XL?

Again, the tell-tale boulder lying in a room is a dead giveaway.

>I would be all in favor of eliminating these inevitable deaths, but I
>don't see any way. 

I don't believe it's possible to have all of;
a) roguelike-style interest through randomly generated situations.
b) a challenging game.
c) no excessively challenging situations that kill you unfairly.

Angband largely eliminates c) through the player's unlimited ability to
retreat without penalty. That largely eliminates b) for cautious play,
save for very deep in the dungeon.

Crawl is a fascinating game because of its high challenge level; but it
indisputably does sometimes kill you without mercy. No-one will ever win
12 in 13 playing Crawl.

NetHack, I think, has a reasonable balance early on, but could do with a
stiffer challenge later - even at the cost of some small proportion of
unavoidable deaths. You still sometimes get enormously sticky situations -
Double Rodney and Demogorgon on a no-tele level springs to mind - but very
infrequently; I'd rather see a higher rate of occurrence of moderately
sticky situations, which the game is bad at generating for high-level
characters.
